Then the strength-famed warrior gave him answer;
the proud-minded chief of the Weders, hardy beneath his helmet,
replied to his question: "We are Higelac's companions;
I am called Beowulf. To the son of Healfdene,
the famous folk-leader, I will freely tell my mission,
to your prince — if he will graciously hear it
and grant that we may greet one so gracious to all men."
Then Wulfgar spoke (he was prince of the Wendels;
his boldness of spirit, his prowess and prudence,
were known to many): "The prince of the Scyldings,

the consort of Hrothgar, mindful of courtesy;
gold-adorned, she saluted the men in the hall,
and the freeborn woman presented the goblet

to whichever side seems proper to him."
Then the brave-minded hero bent down to his slumber;
the pillow received the noble's cheek;

since God did not will it, from getting away —
I did not hold the hated enemy firmly enough;
the foe was too swift. And yet, to save his life,
he left his hand behind him,
his arm and shoulder, as a marker;

The excellent battle-king first brandished his weapon,
the ancient heirloom, with edges unblunted;
for the two death-planners, each was a terror to the other.

Then the warrior-king was careful of glory;
he soundly smote with his sword-for-the-battle,
so that it stood in the head, driven by hatred;
